The strcpy() function is defined in <string.h> and is used to copy one string into another.

Syntax: char *strcpy(char *destination, const char *source);

strcpy(s1, s2) copies the string s2 (source) into s1 (destination), including the null terminator '\0'.

Example:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char s1[20] = "Hello";
    char s2[] = "World";
    strcpy(s1, s2);           // Copies s2 into s1
    printf("%s", s1);         // Output: World
    return 0;
}

After strcpy(s1, s2):
- s1 becomes "World"
- s2 remains "World" (unchanged)

So the answer is b) copies s2 into s1.

